"""Original C1/C2-oriented work on precision, interpretation and mediation."""
from course_authoring import unit

UNITS = [
unit("a05", "Cleft sentences and information focus", "C1", "grammar", "Reorganise a sentence to place contrastive focus deliberately.",
"An it-cleft divides a message: It was the timing that caused the problem. The form foregrounds the timing, often against alternatives. A wh-cleft can foreground an action or need: What we need is a clearer plan. Focus changes the organisation of information without necessarily changing the basic event described. Use emphasis purposefully; too many clefts make prose heavy.",
"Start from The timing caused the problem. Ask what is contrasted: timing, not cost. Move that element after It was and retain the remaining clause. For a wh-cleft, put the information gap first: What we need ... then fill it.",
"""In 'It was the timing that caused the problem', the main focus is: | the timing | the article the | an unnamed person | 0 | The cleft foregrounds the timing.
Choose a well-formed wh-cleft. | What need we a plan is. | What we need is a clearer plan. | What is need we plan. | 1 | The wh-clause is followed by is and the focused complement.
Which emphasises the person who called? | It was yesterday that Kim called. | It was the office that Kim called. | It was Kim who called. | 2 | Kim occupies the focus position.
Why use a cleft? | To organise contrastive focus | To make every sentence passive | To remove all meaning | 0 | Clefts are information-structure choices.
Which focuses on time? | It was Jo who called. | It was on Friday that Jo called. | What Jo needs is help. | 1 | On Friday is foregrounded.
Overusing clefts can: | guarantee elegant style | change all verbs into nouns | make prose unnecessarily heavy | 2 | Emphasis is more effective when selective.
'What surprised me was the silence' foregrounds: | the silence as the surprising element | the exact date | the speaker's occupation | 0 | The complement fills the information gap.""",
"Rewrite a short incident report three times, focusing on the person, time and cause; explain when each version is useful."),
unit("a06", "Ellipsis and substitution", "C1", "discourse", "Avoid repetition while retaining an unambiguous recoverable meaning.",
"Ellipsis omits recoverable material: I can attend, but Jo can't [attend]. Substitution replaces it: I need a pen; do you have one? So can stand for a positive clause after think: I think so. Neither can link a negative parallel: I haven't finished. Neither have I. Recovery must be clear; economy is not a licence to omit necessary meaning.",
"Write the missing words in brackets first. If the reader cannot reconstruct them confidently, restore the phrase. One replaces a countable noun; do so can replace an action in suitable contexts.",
"""'I can attend, but Jo can't' omits: | attend after can't | the subject I | the word but | 0 | The repeated action is recoverable.
'Do you have one?' after 'I need a pen' uses one for: | an entire paragraph always | a pen | an uncountable water | 1 | One substitutes for a singular countable noun.
I haven't finished. — ___ | So have I. | Neither I have. | Neither have I. | 2 | Negative agreement uses neither + auxiliary + subject.
'I think so' can substitute so for: | a positive proposition | any noun in every context | a past participle only | 0 | So can refer to a previously mentioned proposition.
When should omitted material be restored? | Whenever the sentence is short | When recovery would be unclear | Never | 1 | Clarity takes priority over brevity.
Which avoids repetition naturally? | I can attend but Jo can't attends. | I need a pen; do you have a one pen? | I need a pen; do you have one? | 2 | One replaces the repeated singular noun.
Ellipsis relies primarily on: | shared grammatical and discourse context | capital letters | deleting every verb | 0 | Context makes the omission recoverable.""",
"Edit a repetitive dialogue using ellipsis and substitution; annotate exactly what each shortened expression refers to."),
unit("a07", "Participle clauses and logical subjects", "C1", "grammar", "Compress related information without creating a dangling modifier.",
"A participle clause can express background, cause or time economically: Having checked the figures, Maya submitted the report. Its understood subject normally matches the main-clause subject: Maya checked and submitted. 'Walking to work, the rain began' wrongly suggests the rain was walking. A past participle can have passive meaning: Designed for beginners, the course uses clear examples.",
"Name the actor of the opening clause. Having checked ... who checked? The main subject must supply that actor. If it cannot, write a full clause: While I was walking to work, it began to rain.",
"""Who checked the figures in 'Having checked them, Maya submitted the report'? | Maya | the report | nobody identifiable | 0 | The participle clause shares Maya as its subject.
Which avoids a dangling modifier? | Walking home, the rain began. | Walking home, I noticed the rain. | Having read it, the conclusion was obvious. | 1 | I can logically be the person walking.
Having checked normally places checking: | after submission only | in the future only | before the main action | 2 | The perfect participle indicates anterior completion.
'Designed for beginners, the course ...' has: | passive meaning in the opening phrase | a command to beginners | an impossible active subject | 0 | The course was designed for beginners.
The main risk in careless compression is: | adding a clear subject | attaching the phrase to the wrong logical subject | using any punctuation | 1 | A dangling modifier obscures who performs the action.
Choose a clear revision of the walking example. | Walking, rain began to work. | The rain, walking, began. | While I was walking home, it began to rain. | 2 | The full clause states the walker explicitly.
When is a full clause preferable? | When it prevents an unclear subject relationship | Never, because shorter is always better | Only when all verbs are nouns | 0 | Explicitness is useful when compression confuses agency.""",
"Compress three pairs of related sentences into participle clauses, then check and name the subject of each action."),
unit("a08", "Concession and contrast in complex prose", "C1", "discourse", "Express a concession with an appropriate grammatical complement.",
"Although/even though introduce finite clauses: although it was costly. Despite/in spite of take a noun phrase or -ing clause: despite the cost; despite costing more. Nevertheless is a linking adverb, not a substitute for every conjunction. Much as can concede a feeling: Much as I admire the proposal, its risks remain. Concession acknowledges a point without surrendering the main claim.",
"Choose the complement first. Subject + verb → although. A noun → despite. Independent sentence → nevertheless can link it back. Do not write despite it was costly unless using despite the fact that.",
"""Choose the standard construction. | despite the cost | despite it was costly | despite of the cost | 0 | Despite takes a noun phrase here.
Although ___, we continued. | the cost | it was costly | of costing | 1 | Although introduces a finite clause.
Despite ___ more, the option was selected. | it cost | to cost | costing | 2 | Despite can take an -ing clause.
Concession normally does what? | Acknowledges a point while retaining a main claim | Proves both views identical | Removes all disagreement | 0 | The writer recognises an opposing consideration.
Much as I admire it, ... signals: | unconditional endorsement | a concession before qualification | a chronological list only | 1 | Admiration is conceded before the limitation.
Choose a linking-adverb use. | Nevertheless it although costs. | Despite nevertheless the cost. | It was costly. Nevertheless, we continued. | 2 | Nevertheless links two independent sentences.
Which repair is grammatical? | despite the fact that it was costly | despite of it costly | although the cost alone it | 0 | The fact that supplies the noun phrase complement.""",
"Write a balanced recommendation with although, despite and nevertheless; label the conceded point and retained claim."),
unit("a09", "Inverted conditionals", "C1", "grammar", "Recognise and use formal alternatives to if-clauses.",
"Some formal conditional clauses omit if and invert the auxiliary: Had I known ... = If I had known ...; Were it necessary ... = If it were necessary ...; Should you need assistance ... = If you should need assistance ... . The inversion changes style, not automatically the degree of truth. Negatives follow the subject: Had I not checked ... .",
"Expand every inversion into an if-clause before interpreting it. Had I known → If I had known. Should you need → If you should need. Keep the main result clause consistent with the intended time.",
"""Had I known means: | If I had known | Because I certainly knew | When I will know | 0 | This is a formal inverted conditional.
Should you need help means: | You are forbidden help | If you should need help | You needed help yesterday necessarily | 1 | Should introduces a possible future contingency.
Choose a formal negative conditional. | Had not I checked ... | Had I checked not all ... | Had I not checked ... | 2 | Not follows the subject.
Were it necessary means: | If it were necessary | It is always necessary | It was never necessary | 0 | Were inversion corresponds to an if-clause.
What does this inversion mainly affect? | the noun's number | style and clause organisation | all factual evidence | 1 | It is a formal alternative, not proof of truth.
Choose a complete past conditional. | Had I know, I will help. | Had known I, I would help yesterday. | Had I known, I would have helped. | 2 | Had known pairs with a past unreal result.
How should an unfamiliar inverted condition be checked? | Expand it to an if-clause. | Assume it is a question. | Remove its subject. | 0 | Expansion clarifies the underlying condition.""",
"Rewrite three formal contingencies with and without if; explain the time and register of each."),
unit("a10", "Nominalisation, reporting verbs and agency", "C1", "writing", "Choose concise formal phrasing without concealing responsibility.",
"Nominalisation turns an action into a noun: the committee rejected it → the committee's rejection. It can connect complex ideas, but excessive use makes prose dense and may hide the actor. Reporting verbs also encode stance and patterns: admit doing; agree to do; accuse someone of doing. Select a verb that accurately represents the source rather than exaggerating its claim.",
"Recover the actor and action before compressing. 'Implementation was delayed' may need 'The contractor delayed implementation'. Check reporting patterns as whole chunks: admitted making an error; agreed to revise; accused him of copying.",
"""Which makes agency explicit? | The contractor delayed implementation. | A delay occurred. | Implementation experienced delay. | 0 | The actor is named as subject.
She admitted ___ an error. | to make always | making | make to | 1 | Admit can take an -ing complement.
They agreed ___ the draft. | revising only | revise of | to revise | 2 | Agree commonly takes a to-infinitive.
He accused Jo ___ copying. | of | to | for to | 0 | Accuse someone of doing is the pattern.
A risk of excessive nominalisation is: | clearer agency in every case | dense prose with hidden actors | removing every noun | 1 | Actions and responsibility may become harder to identify.
Which is a nominalisation of reject? | rejectedly | rejectingness | rejection | 2 | Rejection is the action noun.
Choose a reporting verb by: | matching its stance and complement pattern to the source | always choosing proved | making every claim stronger | 0 | Attribution should preserve the original commitment.""",
"Rewrite a dense administrative paragraph twice: once for a formal report and once for the public, retaining responsibility and accurate attribution."),
unit("a11", "C2: connotation and fine distinctions", "C2", "vocabulary", "Distinguish evaluative stance from shared denotation.",
"Consider two descriptions: 'The mayor remained resolute' and 'The mayor remained obstinate.' Both may describe persistence, but resolute often approves determination while obstinate often criticises resistance to reason. Thrifty and stingy can likewise share an economy-related core while differing in evaluation. These are tendencies, not fixed verdicts: irony, speaker identity and context may reverse a surface reading. In editing, preserve the evidence separately from the narrator's attitude. A policy can be described as ambitious without proving it is feasible; calling it reckless adds a judgement about risk. At this level, explain why a near-synonym changes stance rather than merely substituting a longer word.",
"Use two columns: observable behaviour and evaluative framing. Kept the policy unchanged → behaviour. Resolute/obstinate → interpretations of that behaviour. Compare a neutral paraphrase before selecting the attitude you intend.",
"""Compared with obstinate, resolute usually conveys: | more approval of persistence | the same disapproval in every context | less persistence as a factual quantity | 0 | Their evaluative tendencies differ despite shared persistence.
Which is closest to a neutral behavioural description? | He was admirably resolute. | He maintained his position after objections. | He was intolerably obstinate. | 1 | It reports the behaviour without the same overt judgement.
Calling a policy reckless adds a judgement about: | its exact cost | its legal status necessarily | the acceptability of its risk | 2 | Reckless evaluates risk-taking negatively.
Why can connotations not be treated as absolute? | Irony and context can change an apparent stance. | Dictionaries contain no information. | Every synonym is identical. | 0 | Interpretation depends on discourse and use.
Which pair most clearly differs in evaluation of economy? | purchased / bought | thrifty / stingy | desk / table | 1 | Thrifty often praises economy; stingy criticises insufficient generosity.
Does ambitious by itself prove feasible? | Yes, because both are positive | Yes, in political texts only | No, scale of aspiration and feasibility are different judgements. | 2 | Ambition does not establish practical achievability.
For precise editing, first separate: | observed conduct from the writer's evaluation | every adjective from all context | spelling from all meaning | 0 | The distinction helps avoid importing an unsupported stance.""",
"Write two 180-word accounts of the same dispute for different editorial stances. Annotate five lexical choices and provide a neutral factual baseline."),
unit("a12", "C2: idiom, register and audience design", "C2", "pragmatics", "Adapt an idiomatic message without losing its implication.",
"A project lead says, 'We have kicked the can down the road long enough.' The expression criticises repeated postponement; a formal paraphrase might be 'We have repeatedly deferred this decision and should now resolve it.' Replacing the idiom with 'We need to discuss the matter' weakens its criticism and urgency. Register adaptation should retain the communicative force while fitting the audience. Highly local idioms can exclude international readers even when grammatical. Familiar metaphors may also conceal assumptions: calling a negotiation a battle frames disagreement as combat. Ask what the phrase implies, who can understand it, and whether that framing is useful.",
"Extract three layers before rewriting: literal topic, speaker's evaluation, requested action. In the postponement example: decision; frustration at delay; resolve now. Keep all three in the new register.",
"""Kick the can down the road usually implies: | postponing a difficult decision | physically measuring a road only | resolving an issue conclusively | 0 | The idiom criticises deferral.
Which formal paraphrase best preserves urgency and criticism? | We have a matter. | Repeated deferral is no longer acceptable; a decision is now needed. | Discussion might someday be interesting. | 1 | It retains the critical stance and immediate need.
The weakest paraphrase is weak mainly because it: | uses fewer syllables | contains no metaphor | loses evaluation and urgency | 2 | Meaning includes communicative force, not only topic.
Why avoid a local idiom for some international audiences? | It may not be shared knowledge. | International readers cannot read formal prose. | Every idiom is incorrect. | 0 | Comprehension depends on familiarity.
Describing negotiation as a battle can frame it as: | a neutral weather report | adversarial competition | guaranteed mutual agreement | 1 | The metaphor brings a conflict frame.
What should register adaptation preserve? | every original word | only the sentence length | the relevant content, stance and intended action | 2 | Appropriate paraphrase retains communicative purpose.
Which is the best first editing question? | What does this phrase imply in this context? | Can I add more rare idioms? | Can I remove the audience? | 0 | Contextual meaning guides successful adaptation.""",
"Adapt a forceful informal project message into a board memo and a message to an international volunteer team. Explain what you retained and softened."),
unit("a13", "C2: ambiguity, scope and precise revision", "C2", "reading", "Identify multiple plausible readings and revise without inventing intent.",
"'The manager discussed the report with the consultant who had arrived late' leaves attachment questions: who arrived late, and is the relative essential to identifying the consultant? 'All the reviewers did not approve' can be read as not all approved or as none approved in some contexts. A responsible editor asks which meaning is intended rather than silently choosing one. 'Only Jo said Kim revised the report' focuses on the speaker, whereas 'Jo said only Kim revised the report' limits the reviser. Small placement changes can alter the scope of a claim. Punctuation may help, but it cannot resolve every ambiguity without added wording.",
"List plausible meanings as separate complete sentences. Then ask the writer to select one. For not all versus none, write those expressions explicitly. Move only next to the constituent its scope should restrict.",
"""Not all reviewers approved means: | at least one did not approve | none approved necessarily | every reviewer approved | 0 | It denies universal approval without specifying that none approved.
None of the reviewers approved means: | some certainly approved | zero reviewers approved | approval is unknown for all | 1 | None excludes every member.
Only Jo said Kim revised it primarily restricts: | the identity of the report | the time of revision | who made the statement | 2 | Only attaches to Jo as the speaker.
Jo said only Kim revised it primarily restricts: | who did the revising | who made the statement | whether the report exists | 0 | Only precedes Kim in the reported content.
When two readings are plausible, an editor should: | choose the preferred meaning silently | seek clarification or present both readings | declare both grammatically impossible | 1 | Revision should not invent the author's intent.
Why might punctuation alone be insufficient? | Punctuation never affects meaning. | Every ambiguity has exactly one comma solution. | Lexical scope can require explicit rewording. | 2 | Some ambiguities concern scope, not merely clause boundaries.
Which wording most clearly states partial non-approval? | Not all reviewers approved the report. | All reviewers did not approve the report. | Reviewers approved not all. | 0 | Not all avoids the more ambiguous negated universal.""",
"Find three potentially ambiguous sentences in a fictional policy. Give two readings of each, then write a revision for each intended meaning."),
unit("a14", "C2: rhetoric, narrative voice and understatement", "C2", "literary_reading", "Interpret a narrator's stance using textual evidence and alternative readings.",
"Read this original miniature: 'The committee celebrated its swift response. It had taken only three winters to replace the broken door. Mara thanked them with the careful warmth usually reserved for a final reminder.' The juxtaposition of swift and three winters invites an ironic reading. Only can intensify understatement rather than literally praise speed. The final comparison suggests controlled irritation, yet the text does not tell us every private motive. Distinguish the narrator's framing, a character's stated words and your inference. A persuasive interpretation cites a detail and explains its effect while allowing a plausible alternative when the evidence remains open.",
"Underline a contrast: swift versus three winters. Name its effect: ironic criticism of delay. Now identify what remains inferred: Mara's irritation is suggested by the comparison, not directly quoted as a confession.",
"""The main source of irony is the contrast between: | swift and three winters | committee and door alone | Mara and a name list | 0 | The claimed speed conflicts with the long delay.
Only three winters most plausibly: | proves the narrator admires efficiency | sharpens criticism through understatement | states that winter lasts one day | 1 | Minimising language makes the delay conspicuous.
Mara's controlled irritation is: | explicitly confessed in direct speech | disproved by the passage | suggested by the final comparison | 2 | The simile supports an inference about her tone.
Which interpretation best uses evidence? | Swift contrasts with three winters, inviting ironic criticism. | The writer dislikes every committee everywhere. | Mara certainly planned revenge. | 0 | It links a textual detail to a proportionate reading.
What should be kept distinct? | page size and font only | narrator framing, character statement and reader inference | all nouns and their lengths | 1 | These represent different levels of evidence and perspective.
The text does not establish: | a broken door existed in the story | the repair took a long time | every private motive Mara had | 2 | Her complete motives are not stated.
A strong interpretation should: | explain effects and acknowledge genuine uncertainty | treat every inference as certain biography | avoid citing details | 0 | Evidence and qualification make the reading defensible.""",
"Write a 220-word close reading of the miniature, including two details, a qualified interpretation and one plausible limitation. Then rewrite it in a sincerely approving voice."),
unit("a15", "C2: critical synthesis and evidential weight", "C2", "mediation", "Integrate competing evidence while distinguishing explanation, limitation and conclusion.",
"Two original summaries: A survey of 40 volunteers links remote work with higher self-reported productivity. A twelve-month company study records unchanged output but fewer absences after a flexible-work policy; staffing also changed during that period. A synthesis should not average these unlike outcomes into a single proof. The measures differ, the populations may differ, and the staffing change complicates attribution. A defensible conclusion is that flexibility may benefit some outcomes, while the evidence here does not establish a general productivity effect. Convergence on one broad theme does not erase disagreements in measurement. Give each claim weight according to its design and relevance, not how strongly it is worded.",
"Create a comparison grid: population, design, measure, finding, limitation. Survey: self-report, small volunteer sample. Company study: recorded output and absence, concurrent staffing change. Synthesis must retain these distinctions.",
"""Why should the two results not be treated as the same measure? | Self-reported productivity and recorded output differ. | Both contain numbers. | Neither mentions any outcome. | 0 | The operational measures are not interchangeable.
What complicates attributing the company's change to the policy? | the study had a title | staffing also changed | twelve months cannot be measured | 1 | A concurrent change is a possible alternative explanation.
Which conclusion best fits the summaries? | Remote work always increases productivity. | Neither study contains any useful information. | Some benefits are possible, but a general productivity effect is not established. | 2 | It acknowledges findings and their limits.
What is a limitation of the volunteer survey? | Small self-selected sample and self-report | It recorded every worker in every country | It used no participants | 0 | These features limit generalisation and interpretation.
How should evidential weight be assigned? | by the strongest adjective used | by design, relevance and limitations | by source title length | 1 | The quality and fit of evidence matter.
Does agreement on a broad theme erase measurement differences? | Yes, always | Only for surveys | No | 2 | Related conclusions can rest on distinct constructs.
Which grid is most useful for synthesis? | population, design, measure, finding, limitation | font, colour, number of headings | adjective length only | 0 | It preserves the distinctions needed for integration.""",
"Write a 250–300-word synthesis of the two summaries, followed by a 100-word plain-language briefing that preserves the uncertainty."),
unit("a16", "C2: mediating disagreement and negotiating meaning", "C2", "interaction", "Reframe conflicting priorities into an accurate, actionable shared understanding.",
"A developer says, 'The system is ready'; an accessibility adviser says, 'It is not usable.' The apparent contradiction may reflect different criteria: feature completeness versus successful use by people with access needs. A mediator should ask each speaker to define the key term, summarise both positions fairly and identify a testable shared next step. 'You agree that everything works' misrepresents the adviser; 'Nothing has been built' misrepresents the developer. A better bridge is: 'Core features are implemented, but accessibility acceptance tests remain unmet. Shall we agree the criteria and test a revised version?' Neutral wording does not mean erasing consequences or giving unsupported claims equal evidential weight.",
"Map term → speaker → criterion → evidence. Ready may mean features implemented; usable may mean accessibility tests passed. Reformulate the difference as an unmet criterion and propose a concrete verification, not a vague demand to agree.",
"""The apparent contradiction may arise from: | different definitions of ready and usable | identical criteria and identical evidence necessarily | a missing date only | 0 | The speakers may be evaluating different conditions.
Which question best clarifies the disagreement? | Who speaks more loudly? | What criteria are you using for ready and usable? | Can we ignore accessibility? | 1 | Defining the terms reveals the evaluative standards.
Which summary represents both positions fairly? | Everything works and everyone agrees. | Nothing has been built. | Features are implemented, but accessibility criteria remain unmet. | 2 | It preserves the distinct claims without erasing either.
What makes a proposed next step actionable? | Agreed acceptance criteria and a test of a revised version | A demand to stop disagreeing | A promise without a criterion | 0 | A concrete test can resolve the practical question.
Neutral mediation should not: | clarify meanings | erase material consequences or misrepresent evidence | identify criteria | 1 | Fairness does not require false equivalence.
Which reformulation misrepresents the adviser? | Accessibility tests remain unmet. | Successful use needs further checking. | You agree that everything works. | 2 | The adviser explicitly disputes usability.
The mediator's central task here is: | make the differences explicit and build a verifiable shared next step | declare one word permanently correct in all contexts | decide by speaking time alone | 0 | Accurate representation enables practical coordination.""",
"Write a 12-turn meeting dialogue resolving this disagreement. Include clarification, two fair summaries, a challenged assumption, acceptance criteria and a negotiated next step."),
]
